:answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394850} :answer-field/id #uuid "788cdc09-4fc8-540a-b4a5-c69fdf43bd48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394848}] :question/id #uuid "50d09abe-1ccb-5c67-80df-655b78fbf493" :question/math-academy-id "q-91192" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{13}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166feab7-a264-55aa-acc5-4218b97966a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412014} {:answer/id #uuid "4c552dee-9870-5f80-99c4-13a42caad182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+3{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+2{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412017} {:answer/id #uuid "99b872c0-2012-5d57-9afd-16e6b6bde53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412018} {:answer/id #uuid "a66ce929-c767-5507-b64f-0b3826324583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412020} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1f46ac-844a-5bb2-b9b6-04cb4156fcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412021} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff26ed5-7a58-584f-95b3-0a0e85d61774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412024}] :question/id #uuid "e8820685-174a-5aac-b0a4-a6b54f80941e" :question/math-academy-id "q-91188" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the right of ${a}_{31}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{1}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3029 :topic/title "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162848} :activity/id #uuid "a251e410-6f92-5d37-91f6-3fe7f0377f20" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163223 :tutorial/content "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a real matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nThe procedure for finding an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ is similar to diagonalizing a regular (diagonalizable) $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nAs an example, consider the matrix \n![](images/ac/acba5373e2f2470930adec855df9958508d9f41aba5d3fc6988b0001c07a9dfd.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. So let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\qquad \\lambda_2=-6, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-9.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** We need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/cd/cddf44571b3aba21dca82cc12664733469c17f30ed3a9a9cfc62acb12230caef.svg)\nNotice that the eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal. \nNow, an orthogonal diagonalization requires each eigenvector to be normalized. So, dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the following unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/e4/e4bfc5b51cea64bd5cb8d340fbeab1a126aaa3f48ae42b991c2b5af2a25480b3.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/4b/4b4a8c98b7c4688fab231510b6b1d615f47bed070310d39f3e812cd9fb811395.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, we automatically get orthogonal eigenvectors because $A$ has three *distinct* eigenvalues. However, in the case of repeated eigenvalues, the corresponding eigenvectors might not be orthogonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "0ff932ad-00a4-58cd-952f-55c21e02ef43" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4881 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a486be1-0f5d-50ea-a245-3aedf2c597b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691"} :step/id #uuid "34cd3b8c-2df0-5886-a6f1-17125ede8748" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5"} :step/id #uuid "e9df74bd-9e6b-5113-a23f-86c30ccc850b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08"} :step/id #uuid "f9795272-744c-5dd3-afc9-73a980ee4bf9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163249 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ad655c9-a00e-5174-a98f-6ae8ab07843d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5013 :tutorial/title "Symmetric Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "63db5c7a-ccd4-5948-af8b-421d4a60d2da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162871}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162215} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505873} :activity/id #uuid "0eb4fea8-7b7f-55ef-a41d-d9f4ac3195c9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357709 :question/id #uuid "22e141ba-7caa-5978-a6dc-ef43c0b1ee42"} :step/id #uuid "7093551b-07fc-5ff1-a4c8-030bdccede09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404693 :question/id #uuid "f0919d70-c1a2-5d35-a661-b7830e88967b"} :step/id #uuid "7f92a652-43f6-587a-a8b8-f62ae03dfd9c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505739 :question/id #uuid "15341cc2-96e2-5aa8-b822-d1195f4412c9"} :step/id #uuid "95bdc2bb-7bd0-5a10-b18b-ed936f4da1af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505766 :question/id #uuid "548a890f-5ff5-5b4e-b39d-7fd5f8a56600"} :step/id #uuid "c90bbda8-2d90-5c6d-95b2-4a5ad309bb13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363196 :question/id #uuid "bf5de7bd-3234-5d41-a9a5-c12fb9a085ef"} :step/id #uuid "edb9db9d-0aa0-5ee6-86cf-b0c3b99d1764"}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505094} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553634} :activity/id #uuid "f3e06349-f9ac-5719-a7b9-44a6d3063919" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492844 :question/id #uuid "2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f"} :step/id #uuid "6ea43364-052a-50ae-ae32-c79c004285a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394963 :question/id #uuid "5bac7e35-18fd-5d08-a4e1-72c5d7aa78ae"} :step/id #uuid "79d57286-dd25-5f24-aefd-354ef80c9c1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492913 :question/id #uuid "d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b"} :step/id #uuid "8b9dd26e-078e-59b0-9131-9d71925a223f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162625 :question/id #uuid "1d443cf2-e450-59f7-912e-8007eb24ac35"} :step/id #uuid "c42ec998-95c8-5b02-9bfd-620df8c32a8c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505739 :question/id #uuid "15341cc2-96e2-5aa8-b822-d1195f4412c9"} :step/id #uuid "c67e89a0-ed38-570d-b6f6-d2395f50c83c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553667}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552867} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555912} :activity/id #uuid "44a916f9-4f4c-59e1-88b3-8921bdc511ff" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394963 :question/id #uuid "5bac7e35-18fd-5d08-a4e1-72c5d7aa78ae"} :step/id #uuid "107f6a9b-7ccb-564e-82de-b78a492059f8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357707 :question/id #uuid "5de03188-13d6-5e04-966b-acf4069cddca"} :step/id #uuid "3d0660e8-f788-5ec5-9a7d-256add4bef95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404700 :question/id #uuid "8750edd7-2f06-5edf-8b79-4b30e6b68324"} :step/id #uuid "419ba880-aac6-5085-bd78-097dd5cf62d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162600 :question/id #uuid "bba46d80-79cd-52e2-b337-86fa5c144249"} :step/id #uuid "6ccb44c9-3756-5059-ae9f-c4960f873716" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555771 :question/id #uuid "20271074-591c-55b9-9da6-42faf357d0f6"} :step/id #uuid "c89c5fdc-99b6-5efd-ace1-eb6840eef7dc"}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554991}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050539 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161780 :question/id #uuid "054b9992-0bf6-5dcc-a04b-038ad6559959" :question/math-academy-id "e-8172" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d054c7daaaeefd4cdf820d49d04d6a0bd544f09e356b859b6291ff7d4588e19.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=0,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_3=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/98/980726b042df523d4c12352fcde141e8c39701a23d72c2815165517dc8ab6949.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ca/caa7100961d28805820530d11708ac6dee747a75b858494d84fcd4c6e5c5d1fb.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c406718-dcb5-57e1-82f4-30ae52dbf5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492430} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7de189-1716-5186-86d1-f361c08a7d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492519} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e7f79b-dd9d-5656-bdf4-163be92b98a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492606} {:answer/id #uuid "cf79b104-5b48-5013-9b3f-1d9e6eb9c9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492628} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4185ef-0279-5e7a-b0f9-ac161094b01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492606} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b62c2ac-065d-54c9-abdd-bbf6c6966cb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160886}] :question/id #uuid "474fce27-4b23-5be8-8b2d-389558be5e18" :question/math-academy-id "q-93727" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6632f3d7-06ae-5c79-b1d4-500c6c3802ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394951} {:answer/id #uuid "e1867d9b-476d-5a5d-89d7-ed8c8216d1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394952} {:answer/id #uuid "8c35dc64-6998-53e6-b3da-d3899a48d48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394953} {:answer/id #uuid "c19d5511-a039-5139-93ab-c5895b1de46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394954} {:answer/id #uuid "451d28cf-25fa-5618-a349-86bebd5f1545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394955} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6651f3-29f1-57b9-8b08-9d44f2e66d1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394950}] :question/id #uuid "dec4c6f4-86dd-50ba-b4f8-5dac075a34ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-94260" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c3a3feb-71ba-5c52-abce-205e4b4c9422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354909} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6fa206-4891-58ec-8206-c202da1d054d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355640} {:answer/id #uuid "78bf6134-458d-56a2-8424-ae22921f1282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355719} {:answer/id #uuid "9826d97e-52fe-5f1b-aced-8b7c432ec7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356008} {:answer/id #uuid "c0be73a0-53b2-5580-a94f-c009f3fc8507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355640} :answer-field/id #uuid "3348c55e-f329-5ac8-9991-b148dc482385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357000}] :question/id #uuid "5de03188-13d6-5e04-966b-acf4069cddca" :question/math-academy-id "q-94257" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1ffe36-0882-5870-a270-de42a9825f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355074} {:answer/id #uuid "30058854-8d3f-57d9-be58-54e0a4a41e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355082} {:answer/id #uuid "47e9181e-3767-5221-bf98-12ae5e9c516c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355303} {:answer/id #uuid "612b21e7-44a4-5ea2-a6d2-221f3d98d3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355528} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6f4c77-e2f7-5a7d-812b-8c47c5918905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5346222-44b8-5c0f-9c03-abcf827a4a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357273}] :question/id #uuid "22e141ba-7caa-5978-a6dc-ef43c0b1ee42" :question/math-academy-id "q-94259" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0566ff51-846c-5cea-a220-d5e08e253a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354699} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb94b32-4966-5900-8f72-af37964940df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354810} {:answer/id #uuid "44c11ea3-6ef7-5621-adf3-5b03d476cf4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355276} {:answer/id #uuid "48845193-ce68-5c8b-9ed2-bc2048595582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355305} {:answer/id #uuid "cde56bcf-834c-56aa-8bd4-9611c6998e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354699} :answer-field/id #uuid "705500ca-8f8b-505a-81a5-3767f669faec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357114}] :question/id #uuid "277e4dcc-7447-5bbf-ae9e-2980066a699d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94300" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3.$ Computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 