mputing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the second and third columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044983dd-f235-51e0-a331-008fc0d161b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540530} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2993d9-6ce2-5240-a5d7-087f9b14447b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540631} {:answer/id #uuid "56942e9a-163a-52c1-a3a7-b6de028f8362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540672} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ddd1b3-cfb4-555f-9ce5-59938f691e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540893} {:answer/id #uuid "e7be7e91-5a14-584b-85a0-f55343f44596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540919} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb2a00c-a32a-58e5-9c52-417ed8779317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541025}] :question/id #uuid "4abf3f5b-ec82-5ed8-874d-b7cd0f7cd357" :question/math-academy-id "q-94348" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462faacd-a6dd-5241-a9c7-f412605afeaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540648} {:answer/id #uuid "8b13dc4d-fe81-56ad-a124-849d6ef3870b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540763} {:answer/id #uuid "8e579e40-44ae-5898-93ae-2dd1031a7f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540766} {:answer/id #uuid "cca46d49-7d23-5e85-ab60-d67226f0d85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540869} {:answer/id #uuid "dc509a11-c0ea-5576-b20f-2da1fad02d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540869} :answer-field/id #uuid "1863d021-7fed-5c34-89fc-7b5b9dc0e6f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540953}] :question/id #uuid "d509446a-8a97-556c-a812-ab4e4c09b27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94343" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find  eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161990 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161782 :question/id #uuid "4779b6be-97c9-5389-b25e-e8f438f0a7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8175" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a21020be45202ebc19fdaeb8d3c334775f072ca037d68805e635e824966d7fd2.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/86/860c02625e5bbb5ac0fcb54e0895274ea38d0462d810da582f75351cbe094b36.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9b3586-3f9f-5bd3-ad90-e25a93828ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354896} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7867c1-d13a-5967-a996-e401c2653e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355019} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdbffeb-b157-541f-b33a-7f8468f27de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355651} {:answer/id #uuid "910ca7ca-e9f0-5190-80d1-362e20a6534e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355946} {:answer/id #uuid "b3da2beb-e835-5c86-80d9-2365e721e1fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354896} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b352c7-da80-5c84-b7fa-8a73e31f0201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357036}] :question/id #uuid "c2f119be-4dbb-5583-8e23-114609166369" :question/math-academy-id "q-93726" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b09121-4945-5fc1-804b-9f45ec3a29ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\fr