/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fb9fc1-f47b-5223-b4a9-520aa22768c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555038} {:answer/id #uuid "20177758-8623-5bdd-95a7-56c8ba5ad1f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555060} {:answer/id #uuid "326e811f-6dc8-5ff1-b97e-d0e1a2b69563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555106} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b99c69-6499-5929-8164-7da16a99cdee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555527} {:answer/id #uuid "f1607c97-1771-5257-80fa-9bb1f93e8de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555060} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9bda7b-a643-53ea-a67f-c84ffc1f798a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555726}] :question/id #uuid "20271074-591c-55b9-9da6-42faf357d0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140831" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{6}{7} & -\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2}{7} & \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3119 :topic/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116124} :activity/id #uuid "0ae25db9-bfdf-5334-a08b-5c0545c2dcde" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116495 :tutorial/content "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix ![](images/09/09dfa5716dbd500a4684f62a5c409a392761e7546c7ac5e4b592133623552506.svg) we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n![](images/44/44bf82527f952ba4d01bbc16f0b81a47fe9e82f447a996f75bccef5171d92242.svg)\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "85735558-3e6a-5a6e-be0b-95227712e60a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3164 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ffae07b2-82f2-5c26-9de5-cb69f517ee2c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f"} :step/id #uuid "7e9e3e32-4737-52d8-8430-910568cdf491" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116496 :tutorial/content "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/01/0144b651cc6eff46265156e1491f58aacfc167da6dce571dbf4a92c9d94e7177.svg)\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/fb/fbb291c4f2d7ccc79939cf9368579b07f6649709f123fb70a1383deefaec6fc1.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/1e/1e71a52dda452f98fa77c01117f40d055fa0661100a0e4ffc25147d9baefc0af.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "24a632e9-4a94-5212-b848-8d4b4403d452" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3165 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d5bdf1c7-f166-5b10-9f1a-0aab443b2ea0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d"} :step/id #uuid "9582140d-32a5-56c0-81bf-8a788ac9965a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116128}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc"} :step/id #uuid "842b6803-5ec6-5291-b4c6-ea03cd8e381f"}] :activity/title "Elementary 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115472}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050576 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115072 :question/id #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" :question/math-academy-id "e-4716" :question/problem "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c89d58a-c775-5607-8754-ada001af1092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113395} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd6d02f-02aa-5e2c-bc02-c7d907a619af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113396} {:answer/id #uuid "68af6deb-1ead-5d87-9d0e-40a52ee56971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113397} {:answer/id #uuid "75472ff7-0158-5d87-bff6-8f21d2b88261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113398} {:answer/id #uuid "7abf9602-3611-5266-a907-f46b7e931769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113399}] :answer-field/id #uuid "38b84383-009a-51c9-ab00-502af2b98f44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113394}] :question/id #uuid "24f9b684-ed46-5b7d-84d3-2382910d4817" :question/math-academy-id "q-31963" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a081c073-7785-5822-a590-7bfe5b3a0d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459993} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a6c82a-ea16-5ff3-8bdf-84c0339a862e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459994} :answer-field/id #uuid "4819ddf4-8130-5b59-a61d-9183708e79f6" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9400fe-5e9b-5eeb-8918-63a684f4e2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459995} {:answer/id #uuid "000331dd-d687-50e7-840d-4298a5368dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459995} :answer-field/id #uuid "234e20cd-9790-5bf2-8ab0-5d985b7db758" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a258fde-3dcf-564c-9b6b-f6a8c2ff2d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459997} {:answer/id #uuid "708f2b4d-d226-5732-a090-fd16b632ea2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459997} :answer-field/id #uuid "12022d23-fdca-5fdc-a369-46e825b1e2e8" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460865}] :question/id #uuid "60c27a10-b6b0-5a54-9d51-0c91bf9fbc6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42591" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $P$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrices $Q$ and $S$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ is an elementary matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82c68fda-0f42-5c48-9715-68ca5d6ebb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463898} {:answer/id #uuid "ae743fce-b45e-5abc-bd86-752ba9bcbc46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463899} :answer-field/id #uuid "01448568-f01a-5eb2-9776-853e263f1da6" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b1bf75-980f-5157-a21e-c5aaa7d1d2bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463900} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2a98f4-65b2-5c86-bfcb-8b40e5c7595b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463901} :answer-field/id #uuid "124e1032-53d8-59c4-9767-8aa9b08c5f27" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11393e6b-38fe-5ee8-a898-8839130cc2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463902} {:answer/id #uuid "1d89d028-e268-579c-869b-ea7d5ab042e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463902} :answer-field/id #uuid "2539df0a-7237-5aec-a2e8-de82405d21b5" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464885}] :question/id #uuid "6ec5e472-dc1d-5891-bb93-866a6d9b3914" :question/math-academy-id "q-130774" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a6fafe-efb0-5881-ae57-4f6578603ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465951} {:answer/id #uuid "a19942b2-5afd-5b36-9805-4daaad62d850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465952} :answer-field/id #uuid "048c186b-3e22-53d2-b5cf-73cb9b7e5280" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466819} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f41dfdf-f241-5b05-b863-2e4f96517509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465953} {:answer/id #uuid "c2318367-e252-5496-9411-127d5517b78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465953} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06f62ee-1262-52be-bf19-60dea19442c8" :answer-field/key "Y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7d46340-1822-5fed-8361-d6d5e3547c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465955} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2bfce5-db39-5ada-92f7-ade37d95ec31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465956} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b4c2f3-46b2-5669-ae14-3227a5a0e6f9" :answer-field/key "Z" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466821}] :question/id #uuid "cbe50951-f70c-5a66-becb-e9afd71bebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31968" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79b0b35e-3c5e-57bc-8aaf-dc4fa16cdba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467524} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd1b03b-3c8d-5a6e-b684-80444b0cc678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467525} :answer-field/id #uuid "de3db739-254c-5821-80ad-9acd32e361f9" :answer-field/key "E" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468483} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3a9014-5808-53b0-ab98-c0cca13bf13b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467526} {:answer/id #uuid "c13400aa-3d88-5366-908f-9f7fffe2af9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467527} :answer-field/id #uuid "e25f4dac-42f6-5106-9850-ab4e5c087959" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7191e2a0-f178-5d00-aec5-334b5b5b342d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467528} {:answer/id #uuid "b4484943-328e-5ce9-9d28-9a215a0a7fd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467528} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3c5d47-0088-54ee-b2a3-b8323cc401fa" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468485}] :question/id #uuid "9df20933-ebce-545d-be35-aa9a6fe179d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-42751" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $Q$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $W$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $E$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d4f8308-7466-5b10-aeb8-3c645cd76aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472658} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef72410-bf24-549a-a67f-ebdeb7154ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472658} :answer-field/id #uuid "db31b420-a5f5-58e8-97fd-f9e9cb76ee53" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bee9e24a-602d-5c0c-98ca-cdd5fbae2bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472660} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7362ee-1b3c-5b7d-b741-5aaa54d8f01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472660} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d9b753-6359-51de-b6d4-0b3f038c56f9" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7057d88b-1459-5572-8b90-225fe7507d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472662} {:answer/id #uuid "09ed49a2-61b4-5571-b71d-a8135e86fa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472663} :answer-field/id #uuid "8936a0e3-4ffe-5d36-8c85-7c84649c2227" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473726}] :question/id #uuid "0d49dd3c-2d26-5daf-baa6-13e98e158d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130772" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c34442a-1ba5-50e6-9956-6c60c53a175d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472664} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb5e1b1-f89e-5daa-85b2-e0dde4da66b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472664} :answer-field/id #uuid "61214d2d-44ba-553b-b6a3-330618cebbc3" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 175921864737