row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $M$, $N$ and $P$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f096b5f-39c2-585d-9076-e381a97ad95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550644} {:answer/id #uuid "95ac74e0-7781-595b-b1cb-5685696d0923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550745} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9ae38d-468e-5c1b-a879-895b482ed7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550766} {:answer/id #uuid "ace569a1-cdae-5f8a-b5cb-eb5a89edde5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550833} {:answer/id #uuid "c605cbc3-363f-5199-936d-cf502df10aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550902} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f0fef4-e8e5-5e33-982a-88aa1db63724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551171}] :question/id #uuid "3a9d38f9-b939-5805-88eb-ef96ffddb725" :question/math-academy-id "q-42754" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $T$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $U$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $X$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $U$ and $X$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2009ffbb-834a-5c7a-8a8b-959aa0b24db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550438} {:answer/id #uuid "40c3d139-ddd9-5cf3-80a9-cb1261eea249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550516} {:answer/id #uuid "44c29b3a-55ca-586c-873f-1a2bc7582a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550528} {:answer/id #uuid "65e51982-290b-5109-8387-cd14e01378eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550619} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a6f7a8-a40b-5e83-8bb3-7321150a3ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550438} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e59a97-7099-5d85-88ab-f4f7fe7124bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551222}] :question/id #uuid "7abe0f71-31aa-5c86-a7a0-fa2df71ee9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42756" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $N$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is not an elementary matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b3907f9-1f0a-5178-9e3a-4ced32f85e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550545} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb9592e-f39c-550c-bee2-e4351328d285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550778} {:answer/id #uuid "ae49d544-bd4f-5e14-976f-0cf55c49ae03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550838} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dfb83d-8ab3-5bf3-b0c6-cf693a27a6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551017} {:answer/id #uuid "fb20b7d7-1969-5e7b-b0bc-77ea754ddb15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551017} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1767fe4-e53c-5579-92c4-0975b0bb4129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551198}] :question/id #uuid "dd52658d-d0dc-5c1e-b084-552fde414f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130775" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115073 :question/id #uuid "40cdd39f-29c3-5dad-9708-02c3e0de6acb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4718" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/03/03d58fbe64af980e170a695b5f511df7e14cd74cc894e802ce5d781642d58b2b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/7b/7bb507644e3602983ed613dbe3b630fbd4fe72799c5412272a072a39c736cbc5.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa76d599-e312-5edc-88d9-382bbf1fad4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113978} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2461e8-8778-528d-a295-5694d36bf193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113979} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d784b-1610-5078-b2e6-78e49eb36316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113980} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcab5d0-7